<JournalArticleLayout
  title="Best Things to Do in Nosara"
  description="The activities we build most Nosara weeks around: surfing Playa Guiones, offshore sportfishing out of Garza, snorkeling Isla Rosada, the Ostional arribada, waterfalls, sunset cruises, yoga, and the art of doing nothing."
  category="Guide"
  authorName="Stacie"
  authorFullName="Stacie Brown"
  heroImage={heroSurf}
  heroImageAlt="A surfer carving a wave with backlit golden spray at Playa Guiones, Nosara, Costa Rica"
  publishedDate="2026-06-18"
  displayDate="June 2026"
  readTime="6 min read"
  slug="best-things-to-do-in-nosara"
>
  <Fragment slot="h1">Best things to do in <em>Nosara</em></Fragment>

  <p class="dropcap">
    When guests ask what there is to do in Nosara, the honest answer is: less
    than you think, and exactly the right things. This is not a town with a
    hundred attractions and a checklist. It is a handful of genuinely great
    experiences and a lot of space to breathe between them. Here is what we
    build most weeks around, in roughly the order people fall for them.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Learn to surf, or get better</h2>
  <p>
    Playa Guiones is one of the most forgiving places in the world to learn,
    a long sand-bottom beach break that works most days of the year. We match
    you to the right instructor and the right tide for your level: a first
    lesson for the kids, a video-review session for someone chasing a better
    bottom turn, or a dawn patrol for the surfer who came for exactly this.
  </p>

  <figure class="journal-body-figure">
    <Image
      src={marlin}
      alt="A marlin jumping clear of the water off the Nosara coast, Costa Rica"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      widths={[500, 720, 1050]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) 95vw, 720px"
    />
    <figcaption>Offshore, a few miles past the break.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>2. Spend a day offshore</h2>
  <p>
    Through our sister company, Monkey Head Adventures, we run boats out of the
    Garza harbor for half and full days on the water. Offshore you are looking
    for marlin, sailfish, tuna, and mahi. Closer in, it is rooster fish and
    snapper. The crew cleans your catch, and the villa chef can cook it for
    dinner that night. Not fishing? The same boats run snorkel trips and sunset
    cruises.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Snorkel the pink island</h2>
  <p>
    A short boat ride from Garza, Isla Rosada sits in water that turns a color
    you do not see from shore. It is the easy, joyful half-day: masks on, kids
    in the water, fruit and cold drinks on the bow, home by lunch. It is usually
    the photo people send first when they get back.
  </p>

  <figure class="journal-body-figure">
    <Image
      src={snorkel}
      alt="A mother and daughter in snorkel masks on a boat during a Nosara ocean day, Costa Rica"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      widths={[500, 720, 1050]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) 95vw, 720px"
    />
    <figcaption>The easiest yes of the week.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>4. See the Ostional arribada</h2>
  <p>
    If your dates line up with a nesting event, less than twenty minutes up the
    coast you can watch tens of thousands of olive ridley turtles come ashore at
    once. It is seasonal and it runs on the moon, not on a schedule, so we watch
    the windows and tell you honestly whether your trip is likely to catch one.
    A certified guide is required by law, through the
    <a href="https://www.wcanosara.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wildlife Conservation Association</a>,
    and we handle the booking.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Find a waterfall</h2>
  <p>
    Behind the beach, the hills hide swimming holes and waterfalls most visitors
    never reach. We arrange the ATVs or the four-wheel-drive, a guide who knows
    which roads are passable that week, and a picnic if you want one. It is the
    best way to see the inland half of the peninsula.
  </p>

  <figure class="journal-body-figure">
    <Image
      src={sandbar}
      alt="A group waving from a boat on a deserted sandbar during a Nosara ocean day, Costa Rica"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      widths={[500, 720, 1050]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) 95vw, 720px"
    />
    <figcaption>A sandbar to yourselves on a boat day.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>6. Take a sunset cruise</h2>
  <p>
    Cold drinks, the light going gold, and dolphins on the way home more often
    than not. In season you may see whales. It is the simplest evening we plan
    and one of the ones people talk about longest.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Spend a morning on wellness</h2>
  <p>
    Nosara has been a yoga town for decades, and the teachers here are the real
    thing. We set up shala classes or private sessions at the villa, plus
    massage, sound healing, and breathwork when you want them. A slow morning
    here resets the whole day.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Eat at the villa with a private chef</h2>
  <p>
    The best dinners in Nosara do not happen in a restaurant. A chef cooks in
    your kitchen with fish landed that morning and produce from up the road,
    around whatever your group is in the mood for. Welcome dinners, a long table
    for a birthday, a quiet plant-based night, kid menus that actually work.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Watch the wildlife</h2>
  <p>
    You barely have to try. Howler monkeys over the pool at dawn, toucans and
    parrots through the day, and a naturalist guide for the morning if you want
    to go deeper into the reserve. Bring binoculars. The kids will use them more
    than you expect.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Do nothing at all</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one we quietly protect on every itinerary. A day with no plan:
    the pool, the beach, a book, a long lunch, a nap. Nosara is a Blue Zone, and
    the doing-nothing is not wasted time here. It is the point.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <p class="eyebrow">Build your days</p>
    <h2>Tell us what you want out of the week</h2>
    <p>
      We respond within twenty-four hours with a tailored proposal: the villa,
      the activities, the boat, the chef, and the space in between. You pick the
      pace.
    </p>
    <div class="journal-article-cta-actions">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn-primary">Plan my trip</a>
      <a href="/experiences" class="btn-ghost">See all experiences</a>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</JournalArticleLayout>
